course: dictionary
lesson: 6667
title: Oar: a boat word with a second meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An oar is a long pole with a flat blade at one end. You push and pull it through the water to row a boat. Context shows you what it means: «Greeks sailed in triremes, fast warships with three rows of oars», so the oars must be the poles that move the ship.
explain: Authors often pick words for a boat, like oars, paddles, sails and rowing. Read the words around the word. «Viking longships were light and fast, with sails and oars» tells you the oars are one way to move a ship, and the sails are another.
explain: Some words have a second meaning. «To stick your oar in» is an idiom, a phrase that means something different from the single words in it. It means giving your opinion when nobody asked for it. If the sentence is about a boat, the word is a noun; if it is about someone talking when they should not, it is the idiom.
fact: An oar is a long pole with a flat blade at one end, used to row a boat.
fact: Oars are held in an oarlock or rowlock on the side of the boat.
fact: A paddle, used in a canoe, is different: it has no fixed place on the boat.
fact: «To stick or put your oar in» means to give your opinion when it is not wanted.
fact: In «Sculling means one person rows with two oars called sculls», a sculler uses two oars at once.
exercise: «Greeks sailed in triremes, fast warships with three rows of oars.» What is an oar? | A rope used to tie a boat up | A sail that catches the wind | *A long pole with a flat blade, used to row a boat | A wide, flat rock on the shore
exercise: Your friend keeps talking when the grown-ups are deciding. You say, «Don't stick your oar in.» What does it mean? | Go and find a paddle | Put the boat in the water | Row the boat faster | *Don't give your opinion when it is not wanted
exercise: Why do rowers face backwards? | *Because pulling the oars towards you is the strongest way to row | Because canoes face backwards | Because they like the view behind them | Because the oars are too heavy to turn around
test: What is the main difference between an oar and a paddle? | *An oar is held in an oarlock or rowlock, but a paddle has no fixed place on the boat | Oars and paddles are exactly the same thing | An oar is used in a canoe and a paddle is used in a rowing boat | An oar has no blade, but a paddle does
test: «Sculling means one person rows with two oars called sculls.» How many oars does one sculler use? | Four | One | *Two | Three
exam: Which sentence uses oar with its correct meaning? | The children rowed the oar to school every morning | Please don't put your oar in while we are talking, it is rude | The oar was a loud noise in the classroom | *Our team pulled hard on the oars, and the boat shot across the river
